{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GCWJqyEZ8kj4gELqHotsaV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel02.2-uc002-seq\n     2→title: \"Print a sequence of numbers via seq\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator invokes the Go seq binary to generate a sequence of numbers for shell\n     6→  scripting and pipeline use. The differential testing harness runs both the Go binary\n     7→  and the Homebrew reference binary (gseq from coreutils) with the same arguments and\n     8→  environment, then compares stdout byte-for-byte.\n     9→\n    10→actor: Human Operator\n    11→\n    12→trigger: |\n    13→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd019-seq.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n    14→  a passing differential test for cmd/seq.\n    15→\n    16→flow:\n    17→  - id: F1\n    18→    step: |\n    19→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 5\". seq prints lines 1 through 5, one per line.\n    20→  - id: F2\n    21→    step: |\n    22→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 2 5\" to print a range from 2 to 5.\n    23→  - id: F3\n    24→    step: |\n    25→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 1 2 10\" to print odd numbers from 1 to 9 with\n    26→      step 2.\n    27→  - id: F4\n    28→    step: |\n    29→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 5 -1 1\" to print a descending sequence from 5 to 1.\n    30→  - id: F5\n    31→    step: |\n    32→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq -w 8 12\" to print zero-padded numbers 08 through 12.\n    33→  - id: F6\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq -f '%.2f' 1 3\" to print numbers with two decimal\n    36→      places.\n    37→  - id: F7\n    38→    step: |\n    39→      The operator runs \"./bin/seq -s ', ' 1 3\" to print numbers separated by commas\n    40→      instead of newlines.\n    41→  - id: F8\n    42→    step: |\n    43→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/seq and gseq\n    44→      with the same arguments and environment.\n    45→  - id: F9\n    46→    step: |\n    47→      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte and exit codes. Both binaries must\n    48→      produce identical output.\n    49→\n    50→touchpoints:\n    51→  - T1: \"cmd/seq — argument forms, sequence generation, formatting (prd019-seq R1, R2, R3)\"\n    52→  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, stdout comparison\"\n    53→\n    54→success_criteria:\n    55→  - id: S1\n    56→    criterion: cmd/seq compiles and ./bin/seq exists after mage build.\n    57→  - id: S2\n    58→    criterion: |\n    59→      \"seq 5\" produces lines 1 through 5, matching gseq.\n    60→  - id: S3\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      Two-argument form works: \"seq 2 5\" produces lines 2 through 5.\n    63→  - id: S4\n    64→    criterion: |\n    65→      Three-argument form works: \"seq 1 2 10\" produces 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n    66→  - id: S5\n    67→    criterion: |\n    68→      Descending sequences work: \"seq 5 -1 1\" produces 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.\n    69→  - id: S6\n    70→    criterion: |\n    71→      Equal-width padding works: \"seq -w 8 12\" produces 08, 09, 10, 11, 12.\n    72→  - id: S7\n    73→    criterion: |\n    74→      Format strings work: \"seq -f '%.2f' 1 3\" produces 1.00, 2.00, 3.00.\n    75→  - id: S8\n    76→    criterion: |\n    77→      Custom separators work: \"seq -s ', ' 1 3\" produces \"1, 2, 3\" on one line.\n    78→  - id: S9\n    79→    criterion: |\n    80→      Error cases exit 1: zero step, invalid format, non-numeric arguments.\n    81→  - id: S10\n    82→    criterion: |\n    83→      The differential test passes for all argument forms, -w, -f, -s, descending,\n    84→      floating-point, and error cases.\n    85→\n    86→out_of_scope:\n    87→  - Hexadecimal integer output is excluded; use -f '%a' for hex floating-point.\n    88→  - Infinity (inf) as an endpoint is excluded; sequences must be bounded.\n    89→  - Locale-aware decimal separators are excluded; output always uses '.'.\n    90→\n    91→test_suite: test-rel02.2\n    92→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"930bb9c0-57e2-4c3f-8188-10a0cdccbab8","uuid":"6ae3eb6b-20ee-482b-b922-2a95698ffdf4","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/344/docs/specs/use-cases/rel02.2-uc002-seq.yaml","content":"id: rel02.2-uc002-seq\ntitle: \"Print a sequence of numbers via seq\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator invokes the Go seq binary to generate a sequence of numbers for shell\n  scripting and pipeline use. The differential testing harness runs both the Go binary\n  and the Homebrew reference binary (gseq from coreutils) with the same arguments and\n  environment, then compares stdout byte-for-byte.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd019-seq.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n  a passing differential test for cmd/seq.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 5\". seq prints lines 1 through 5, one per line.\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 2 5\" to print a range from 2 to 5.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 1 2 10\" to print odd numbers from 1 to 9 with\n      step 2.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq 5 -1 1\" to print a descending sequence from 5 to 1.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq -w 8 12\" to print zero-padded numbers 08 through 12.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq -f '%.2f' 1 3\" to print numbers with two decimal\n      places.\n  - id: F7\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"./bin/seq -s ', ' 1 3\" to print numbers separated by commas\n      instead of newlines.\n  - id: F8\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/seq and gseq\n      with the same arguments and environment.\n  - id: F9\n    step: |\n      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte and exit codes. Both binaries must\n      produce identical output.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/seq — argument forms, sequence generation, formatting (prd019-seq R1, R2, R3)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, stdout comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/seq compiles and ./bin/seq exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      \"seq 5\" produces lines 1 through 5, matching gseq.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      Two-argument form works: \"seq 2 5\" produces lines 2 through 5.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      Three-argument form works: \"seq 1 2 10\" produces 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      Descending sequences work: \"seq 5 -1 1\" produces 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      Equal-width padding works: \"seq -w 8 12\" produces 08, 09, 10, 11, 12.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      Format strings work: \"seq -f '%.2f' 1 3\" produces 1.00, 2.00, 3.00.\n  - id: S8\n    criterion: |\n      Custom separators work: \"seq -s ', ' 1 3\" produces \"1, 2, 3\" on one line.\n  - id: S9\n    criterion: |\n      Error cases exit 1: zero step, invalid format, non-numeric arguments.\n  - id: S10\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for all argument forms, -w, -f, -s, descending,\n      floating-point, and error cases.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - Hexadecimal integer output is excluded; use -f '%a' for hex floating-point.\n  - Infinity (inf) as an endpoint is excluded; sequences must be bounded.\n  - Locale-aware decimal separators are excluded; output always uses '.'.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel02.2\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}}}
